## Source roots, test roots and your layout

`Source roots`, `Test root` and `Shared surfaces` are comma-separated paths,
relative to the repo root. Plain paths (`src`, `app/Http`, `WebSite`) mean
themselves. `*` is allowed as a **whole path segment** and matches exactly one
directory name, never a slash:

| Layout | Source roots | Test root |
|---|---|---|
| Single app | `src` | `tests` |
| Monorepo (pnpm/yarn/npm workspaces, Nx, Turbo, Cargo/Go workspaces) | `apps/*/src, packages/*/src` | `apps/*/tests, packages/*/tests` |
| Packages that keep code at their own root | `packages/*` | (leave blank: test files match by name) |
| Maven / Gradle / sbt | `src/main` | `src/test` |
| Rails | `app, lib` | `spec` |
| Next.js-style, no `src/` | `app, components, lib` | `tests` |
| Your own structure | every directory that holds code you want planned first | wherever your tests live |

A `*` entry guards packages added later without editing this file. `src*`,
`**`, `?` and a bare `*` are refused: you could not read off the profile what
they reach. A test root inside a source root is dropped with a warning,
because an allow rule there would ungate the root; test FILES are still
recognised by name.

`configure.sh` lists tracked code that no root covers. Leaving a directory out
is fine when it is a decision (scripts, tooling). After configuring, run
`bash .claude/scripts/layout-map.sh` and read
`.claude/skills/codebase-map/layout.md`: it shows every directory the roots
actually matched, which is the quickest way to see the profile says what you
meant.

## Shared surfaces

Comma-separated directories, relative to the repo root, where a NEW file has to
state what it reuses before it is created -- components, pages, services: the
places a fifth near-duplicate of an existing thing tends to appear. Editing an
existing file in them is never gated.

Leave blank to switch the reuse gate off. That is a decision, not a default:
"reuse what is already here" stays advisory until something asks.

## Extra test patterns

The built-in test-filename shapes (`*.test.*`, `*.spec.*`, `*_test.*`,
`test_*`, `*Test.php`, `*Spec.php`, `*.Tests.cs`, `*Tests.cs`, `*Test.cs`,
`conftest.py`) cover Go, Rust, most JS/TS layouts, PHP and .NET's two common
test-suffix conventions. A stack with its own convention this list does not
know -- `*_spec.rb`, `*.feature`, whatever this project actually uses --
needs it named here, comma-separated, or the TEST phase cannot write its own
failing test: the file it just created reads as ordinary source and blocks
like any other.

SQL and reporting roots usually have no filename convention a hook could
recognise as a test at all. That is expected, not a gap to fill here --
those slices normally record `--red "n/a: <reason>"` instead of a real test
file, exactly like a design token with no behaviour to pin.

Leave blank if the built-in list already covers this stack.

## Worktrees

Each `git worktree` has its **own** `.claude/state/gate.json`, and hooks judge
a file by the nearest one walking up from that file, not by the main
checkout's. Two consequences:

- Run `bash .claude/scripts/gate.sh ...` from **inside** the worktree you are
  working in. Running it from the main checkout only ever opens or closes the
  main checkout's gate, never a worktree's.
- A new worktree starts with no gate file at all, which reads as `idle` --
  guarded source stays blocked there until you open it, exactly as a fresh
  clone would.

